  "html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\">\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.gstatic.com/\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\">\n    <link rel=\"preload\" as=\"style\" onload=\"this.rel='stylesheet'\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins&display=swap\">\n\n    <title>BEAUTY BAY: The destination to explore your beauty obsessions</title>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <!--\n      Prerender.io readiness gate. Start \"not ready\" so prerender.io waits for the\n      active page to signal completion via setPreRenderReadyState(true) — which each\n      SEO page (PDP -> Product/ErrorPage, lister, home, static, 404) calls only AFTER\n      its data resolves. Without this, window.prerenderReady is undefined at load, so\n      prerender.io falls back to its network-idle heuristic and can snapshot a\n      still-loading (thin) DOM, which Google classifies as a soft 404.\n      Must run before the app bundle so the flag is false the moment the page loads.\n    -->\n    <script>\n      window.prerenderReady = false;\n    </script>\n    <link\n      rel=\"preload\"\n      async\n      href=\"https://use.typekit.net/zjs2kuv.css\"\n      as=\"style\"\n      onload=\"this.onload=null;this.rel='stylesheet'\"\n    />\n    <noscript>\n      <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://use.typekit.net/zjs2kuv.css\" />\n    </noscript>\n    <meta http-equiv=\"X-UA-Compatible\" content=\"IE=Edge;chrome=1\" />\n    <meta\n      name=\"viewport\"\n      content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no, minimum-scale=1\"\n    />\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#ffffff\" />\n    <base href=\".\" />\n\n    <link rel=\"manifest\" href=\"/manifest.json\" />\n    <link rel=\"shortcut icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\" />\n    <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" sizes=\"180x180\" href=\"/apple-touch-icon.png\" />\n    <link\n      rel=\"apple-touch-icon-precomposed\"\n      sizes=\"180x180\"\n      href=\"/apple-touch-icon-precomposed.png\"\n    />\n    <meta name=\"msapplication-TileColor\" content=\"#ffffff\" />\n    <meta\n      name=\"facebook-domain-verification\"\n      content=\"qqbroa8sa4ka9pi2e8tlajchicj75a\"\n    />\n    <meta\n      name=\"facebook-domain-verification\"\n      content=\"wzie96286hk90x29r74bikonndkxyg\"\n    />\n    <meta name=\"p:domain_verify\" content=\"b14036d1e1ae0ae6145c763c770a89e2\" />\n\n    <link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://images.beautybay.com/\" crossorigin />\n    <link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://images.ctfassets.net/\" crossorigin />\n    <!-- Product API: warm DNS+TLS so the PDP's product fetch (which yields the\n         LCP image URL) doesn't pay a cold connection on first navigation. -->\n    <link\n      rel=\"preconnect\"\n      href=\"https://pdp-api.public.prd.beautybay.com\"\n      crossorigin\n    />\n    <!--\n      The two origins the homepage BLOCKS on, which had been missed while the\n      PDP's was handled above.\n\n      home-api serves the route loader (getNewHomePageContent). React Router\n      will not render the route until it resolves, and the LCP element — the\n      promo banner — comes from it, so nothing paints until this returns.\n      graph.beautybay.com is the header's GraphQL query, which decides the nav\n      and the banner above it.\n\n      Neither can start until the bundle has booted, measured at ~3.0s and\n      ~3.5s into a throttled mobile load. Both then pay a full cold connection\n      at that point: on Lighthouse's mobile profile (150ms RTT) DNS + TCP + TLS\n      is roughly three round trips, which matches home-api taking 1085ms there\n      against 240ms unthrottled.\n\n      Preconnecting opens both while the HTML is still parsing, so the\n      handshake is done long before the app asks. The gap is ~3s, comfortably\n      inside the browser's idle-connection window.\n    -->\n    <link\n      rel=\"preconnect\"\n      href=\"https://home-api.public.prd.beautybay.com\"\n      crossorigin\n    />\n    <link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://graph.beautybay.com\" crossorigin />\n\n    <!-- Inter Tight font (sitewide) -->\n    <link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com\" />\n    <link\n      rel=\"preload\"\n      as=\"font\"\n      type=\"font/woff2\"\n      href=\"https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/intertight/v9/NGSwv5HMAFg6IuGlBNMjxLsH8ahuQ2e8.woff2\"\n      crossorigin\n    />\n    <!--\n      Loaded without blocking render, the same way the Typekit stylesheet above\n      is. As a plain `rel=\"stylesheet\"` this cost a round trip to\n      fonts.googleapis.com before first paint — Lighthouse attributed ~56ms of\n      render-blocking to it, and it showed up in the critical window at ~169ms\n      on a throttled load.\n\n      Deferring the CSS does not change what the viewer sees: `display=swap` is\n      already set, so text has always painted in the fallback face first, and\n      the woff2 for the common weight is preloaded above so the swap is not\n      delayed by discovering it late.\n\n      Left pointing at Google rather than self-hosted: the family resolves to 49\n      @font-face blocks (7 weights x 7 subsets — latin, latin-ext, cyrillic,\n      greek, greek-ext, vietnamese), and Google serves the right subset per\n      browser. Self-hosting would mean committing all of them and hand-marking\n      the unicode-ranges, which risks losing glyphs in the non-Latin locales.\n    -->\n    <link\n      rel=\"preload\"\n      as=\"style\"\n      href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter+Tight:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800;900&display=swap\"\n      onload=\"this.onload=null;this.rel='stylesheet'\"\n    />\n    <noscript>\n      <link\n        href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter+Tight:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800;900&display=swap\"\n        rel=\"stylesheet\"\n      />\n    </noscript>\n\n    <script\n      type=\"text/javascript\"\n      src=\"//widget.trustpilot.com/bootstrap/v5/tp.widget.bootstrap.min.js\"\n      async\n    ></script>\n    <!-- Constructor.io behavioral tracking config.\n\n         `window.cnstrc.indexKey` is no longer hard-coded here: the index now\n         depends on environment AND locale (one build serves every language), so\n         it is resolved in src/app/shared/utils/constructorIndexKey.ts and set\n         from the entry module. That module is deferred, so it runs before the\n         `load` handler below that injects the beacon.\n\n         Index (API) keys are PUBLIC — they are visible in the beacon URL. The\n         catalogue API token is a real secret and must never appear here. -->\n    <!--\n      Loaded after the window load event rather than as a parser-deferred\n      script. `defer` still runs before DOMContentLoaded, so on mobile the\n      beacon was starting at 368ms and pulling 134.8KB — earlier than GTM\n      (700ms), which already waits for load — right while the app's own bundle\n      and data are competing for a throttled connection.\n\n      Safe to move: the beacon is behavioural, reading data-cnstrc-* attributes\n      off the DOM (search forms, autosuggest, PDP and lister tiles). Nothing\n      renders those before load, and `window.cnstrc.indexKey` is set by the\n      deferred entry module, which always executes before this `load` handler.\n\n      Deliberately tied to `load` and not to user interaction — unlike the\n      TikTok pixel below. Constructor drives search personalisation, so it\n      should be ready before someone opens search rather than being triggered\n      by it.\n    -->\n    <script>\n      window.addEventListener(\"load\", function () {\n        var s = document.createElement(\"script\");\n        s.src = \"https://cnstrc.com/js/cust/beauty_bay_OVJ-3u.js\";\n        s.async = true;\n        document.head.appendChild(s);\n      });\n    </script>\n    <!-- TikTok Pixel Code Start -->\n    <script>\n      !function (w, d, t) {\n        w.TiktokAnalyticsObject=t;var ttq=w[t]=w[t]||[];ttq.method